# TODO - EEPROM/OTP

## Objective

Complete bounded NVM read algorithms, dictionaries, revision matching,
MAC/board checksums, antenna and calibration data interpretation, with safe
rejection of unknown layouts. Use ath9k references; **never require a dump of
the user's actual EEPROM to finish the source task**.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Validate NVM checksums, board identity and reject unknown layouts.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell test.

## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Connect bounded EEPROM and OTP acquisition, source/origin discovery,
      byte ordering and reconstruction to the native owner for all three
      AR9565 revisions. Preserve timeout/protection/absence error distinctions.
- [ ] Reconstruct the full board image with verified lengths/checksums and
      exact dictionaries. Reject malformed/unknown layouts and invalid MACs;
      never replace missing board data with a template or spoofed MAC.
- [ ] Interpret calibration piers, antenna/diversity, gain indexes, board
      flags, RF-kill, regulatory and power fields consumed by RF/PHY source.
      A raw summary or readable control register does not validate NVM data.
- [ ] Define immutable board-data lifetime, failure erasure and reset/resume
      invalidation; connect consumers to validated board state in code.

## Start here and next implementation step

Use captureAr9565Nvm and discoverAr9565Nvm in Ar9565NvmReader.*,
restoreEepromDump/applyEepromBlock in Ar9565Eeprom.*, and the five pinned
reconstruction dictionaries. Commits 904b0e1 and 9cfc45b implement bounded
EEPROM/OTP first-nonempty-header origin search, source provenance, image
reconstruction, and a revocable owner-epoch NvmBoardLease; synthetic offline
regressions cover error paths, stale epochs and invalidation. These are portable
source components: no native PCI owner invokes them, no board calibration is
validated for RF, and no live EEPROM has been read. BoardDataSummary is not
calibrated RF state.

The source/origin algorithm is grounded in ar9300_eeprom_restore_internal in
[ar9003_eeprom.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_eeprom.c).
Next, connect the acquisition/lease to a verified exclusive native PCI owner
and calibrated RF consumers, implement all required board-data interpretation,
and revoke/drain outstanding work on power loss and teardown. Keep source kind,
origin, byte order and accepted block provenance with the immutable image.
The EEPROM/OTP search spans are bounds, not discovered physical capacities.
Never synthesize usable board data from the default dictionary alone.

The passive AR_EEPROM control register is 0x401c with its absent/corrupt flags;
the AR9565 active EEPROM status/data register is 0x4084. They are distinct,
legitimate registers. Reading either control/status value is not a full-image
checksum or calibration check. Complete the board/calibration consumers used by
radio/power code and invalidate them on source/reset failure.

Existing host regression tests/test_nvm_contract.cpp covers second-read payload
order, post-ready faults, output wiping and a regressing clock. Further useful
cases: reconstruction truncation/checksum failure, overlapping output storage,
unsupported gain indexes and valid templates with invalid board restrictions.

## Evidence

- Source commits, reference URLs/version/rights, static review and lint diagnostics.
- Theoretical completeness only; no kext build, device scan, packet logs or hardware claims.
- Add offline host tests only where material behavior/error paths warrant them;
  run through tools/validate.py --tests without treating them as hardware proof.
